  Task: read string s (lowercase letters, length 1..1e5), read x and y, replace all x with y, output result.

  Algorithm: read line with getline (careful about possible trailing '\r' from CRLF line endings — need to strip trailing '\r'), then read x and y.

  Edge cases: input s line may end with CRLF; if we read with `cin >> s` it handles whitespace skipping fine — since s consists only of lowercase letters, `cin >> s` is safe and handles trailing \r (as whitespace). That's simpler and robust. Then `cin >> x >> y`.

  But careful: `cin >> s` skips leading whitespace — fine since s has no whitespace.

  So:

  ```cpp
  #include <bits/stdc++.h>
  using namespace std;
  int main(){
      ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
      cin.tie(nullptr);
      string s; char x, y;
      if(!(cin >> s)) return 0;
      cin >> x >> y;
      for(char &c : s) if(c == x) c = y;
      cout << s << '\n';
      return 0;
  }
  ```

